  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Friday)\nIssued at 1256 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nOur active synoptic pattern keeps on truckin' into early next week, \nas models have come into much better agreement regarding another \nsystem set to reach the Panhandles Mon-Tue. Data predominantly \nsuggests Monday's weather will be mostly dry and quiet in between \nsystems, with slightly breezy winds and highs in the 40s. As the \nnext wave draws near though, mid-level moisture will be advected to \nthe region Monday night into Tuesday. Once the cold core and main \nlift from the trough reach the Panhandles, ensemble and \ndeterministic guidance provide better indications that there will be \na brief window for scattered precipitation to develop (20-50% POPs \npeaking Mon night). Temperature profiles in forecast soundings favor \nprecipitation initially falling as rain, but a transition to a \nrain/snow mix or all snow may occur as wet-bulb processes take \nplace. \n\nAs we finally start to gain a relatively clearer picture of what to \nexpect from this system, it's important to note that this is more \nor less the first window of model agreement we've seen so far. While \ndata elements point to winter precipitation chances for the area \nnow, trends could still easily shift this potential away from the \nPanhandles, or trend warmer/colder. One consistent theme we've seen \nis that impactful wintry precipitation still appears highly \nunlikely, especially given how warm our ground temps have been. With \nthat being said, if we are able to attain colder temperature \nprofiles for snow earlier in the event and achieve locally heavier \nsnowfall rates, there could be a corridor somewhere in the \nPanhandles that sees a short-lived dusting to perhaps even an inch \nof snow by Tuesday (10-30% chance). \n\nRegardless, the system will exit to our east through the day \nTuesday, purging any appreciable moisture while leaving us cool and \ndry. Hard freezes in the morning are looking likely mid next week, \nso be prepared to protect any outdoor pets and plants. Daytime temps \nwill gradually warm through the Thanksgiving holiday stretch, but \nstay near average for late November. Long range global model \nguidance continue to trend pessimistically regarding a late week \nsystem, tracking it well south of the region on a consistent basis. \nEarly indications are that impacts to peak holiday travel next week \nshould be slim to none, but please continue to monitor forecast \nupdates as we approach Thanksgiving. \n\nHarrel",
